# Backend Fundamentals and APIs: The Server Behind the Screen

> Every "Check availability" button and every feed refresh is a
> conversation: the browser asks, a distant server answers. Frontend
> code (HTML, CSS, JavaScript) builds what users see; the
> **backend** — servers, routes, and APIs — does the remembering,
> checking, and deciding. This lesson explains how that conversation
> works and how to build your first API.

## 1. Client, Server, and the Request Cycle

[definition]
The **backend** is the server-side layer of an application: it
receives HTTP requests, applies business logic, reads and writes a
database, and sends responses. The **frontend** (browser or app) is
the client that initiates requests.
[/definition]

One click triggers a familiar loop:

1. The client builds an **HTTP request**: method + URL + headers +
   optional body.
2. DNS resolves the hostname; the request travels to the server.
3. The server's **router** matches the URL to a handler.
4. The handler runs business logic, usually talking to a database.
5. The server replies with a **status code, headers, and a body**
   (today, nearly always JSON).
6. The client renders the result — and the cycle repeats.

## 2. HTTP: Method, URL, Status

| Method | Meaning | Typical use | Body? |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| GET | read | fetch a list or item | no |
| POST | create | submit a new order | yes |
| PUT / PATCH | replace / partially update | edit a profile | yes |
| DELETE | remove | cancel an item | usually no |

Status codes form a small vocabulary worth memorising:

- **2xx success** — 200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content
- **3xx redirect** — 301 Moved Permanently
- **4xx client error** — 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized,
  403 Forbidden, 404 Not Found, 429 Too Many Requests
- **5xx server error** — 500 Internal Server Error, 503 Unavailable

[important]
Idempotency separates the methods: GET, PUT, and DELETE repeated
give the same result as once; POST does not — re-submitting a POST
creates a second order. This is why browsers warn before
re-submitting forms, and why payment buttons must guard against
double clicks.
[/important]

## 3. REST: Resources Speak in Nouns

**REST (Representational State Transfer)** is the dominant API
style: URLs name *resources* (nouns), HTTP methods express the
*verb*, and JSON carries the payload.

```text
GET    /api/books          list books
GET    /api/books/42       one book
POST   /api/books          create (body carries data)
PATCH  /api/books/42       update fields
DELETE /api/books/42       remove
```

A well-designed REST API can be read aloud: "GET book 42", "DELETE
review 7". Contrast with RPC-style URLs like `/getBook?id=42` —
workable, but REST's uniformity lets tools, caches, and other
developers predict everything.

## 4. A Minimal API in Node.js (Express)

Node.js with Express is the smallest complete backend in common use:

```javascript
const express = require("express");
const app = express();

app.use(express.json());              // parse JSON request bodies

let books = [{ id: 1, title: "Panchatantra" }];

app.get("/api/books", (req, res) => {
  res.json(books);
});

app.post("/api/books", (req, res) => {
  const book = { id: books.length + 1, title: req.body.title };
  books.push(book);
  res.status(201).json(book);         // 201 = Created
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log("API on :3000"));
```

Three routes in fifteen lines — yet the shape (middleware to parse
bodies, handlers receiving `req`/`res`, JSON responses with
deliberate status codes) scales unchanged to production services in
any framework: Flask (Python), Spring (Java), Laravel (PHP), Rails
(Ruby).

## 5. The Frontend Calls Back: fetch

From the browser, the `fetch` API makes the same conversation
asynchronous — the page never freezes while awaiting the server:

```javascript
const res = await fetch("/api/books", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({ title: "Hitopadesha" })
});
const created = await res.json();     // parse the JSON reply
```

`async/await` (introduced with promises) keeps asynchronous code
reading top-to-bottom while the event loop stays free.

## 6. Statelessness, Auth, and Sessions

HTTP itself remembers nothing — every request arrives anonymous.
Backends add memory through:

- **Sessions:** a cookie stores a random ID; the server keeps a
  table of ID → user. Simple, but state lives on one server (or a
  shared store).
- **Tokens (JWT):** the client holds a signed token and sends it in
  the `Authorization` header; the server verifies the signature
  without a lookup table — the standard for APIs and mobile apps.
- **API keys / OAuth:** machine-to-machine access and
  "log in with Google" flows.

[example]
A login flow ties the pieces together: the client POSTs credentials
to `/api/login`; the server verifies them (hashing passwords with
bcrypt — never storing plaintext), then signs a JWT containing the
user ID and an expiry. Every later request carries
`Authorization: Bearer <token>`; the server checks the signature and
expiry, and knows who is asking without any server-side session
state.
[/example]

## 7. Beyond the Basics

- **Databases behind APIs:** handlers translate requests into queries
  (SQL or NoSQL); the database, not the server, is usually the
  scaling bottleneck.
- **Validation and sanitisation:** never trust client input —
  validate types, lengths, and permissions on the server, because
  anyone can call your API with a tool, bypassing your UI.
- **CORS:** browsers block cross-origin AJAX calls unless the server
  opts in with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` headers.
- **GraphQL and friends:** an alternative style where clients
  describe exactly the shape of data they want, in one request.
- **Environment and secrets:** ports, database URLs, and keys belong
  in environment variables — never committed to the repository.

[summary]
The backend receives HTTP requests, applies logic, touches a
database, and answers with JSON and a status code. Methods carry
intent — GET reads, POST creates, PUT/PATCH updates, DELETE removes
— with POST alone being non-idempotent. REST names resources as
nouns in URLs so APIs stay predictable; a fifteen-line Express app
demonstrates the whole pattern (middleware, handlers, status-correct
JSON replies), and the browser joins in with `async/await fetch`.
Statelessness is patched by sessions or signed JWTs, while
production readiness adds validation, CORS, secrets in environment
variables, and a database chosen for the workload. Frontend is what
users see; the backend is what makes it true.
[/summary]
